 "interpretation": [
  {
   "heading": "1. Honest training costs nothing measurable",
   "body": "Honest map 5.87 vs privileged control 5.93: inside the noise of single runs (one run moved 1.08 between its own checkpoints). Same with WAKE_UP removed: 4.90 vs 5.03. The map no longer learns from terrain the agent never saw, and the score did not drop."
  },
  {
   "heading": "2. The pickaxe: the fixes are probably not the cause",
   "body": "Control: pickaxe 0%, sword 60%. Honest map, same recipe and death fixes: pickaxe 20%, sword 20%. Old record, without the fixes: pickaxe 30%, sword 10%. If the death fix had removed the pickaxe's incentive, the honest run should have lost it too. That leaves (a) run-to-run variance and (b) valid-action suppression -- both crafts are valid in the same state, and which key survives looks close to arbitrary per run. The honest run also differs from the control in its map targets, so this weakens rather than rules out (c)."
  },
  {
   "heading": "3. The map model's gain is mostly sleep",
   "body": "WAKE_UP fires in 83-98% of episodes for every map run that learned to sleep, and 0% for vanilla. Remove it and the map model alone (4.20) is below vanilla (4.44). The potential is what adds tech progress. Why map runs learn to sleep is an open question; it is systematic, not seed noise."
  },
  {
   "heading": "4. Survival is still unsolved",
   "body": "Every run lives 243-294 steps, inside or below the random band. The honest run drinks most reliably (83%) yet dies earliest, half the time to mobs or lava. Thirst is no longer its main killer; combat is."
  },
  {
   "heading": "5. What to run, cheapest first",
   "body": "Experiment A: probability of each crafting key in table-with-wood states, on the control, the honest run and the old record (no training). Then second seeds, which are the only way to separate (a) from (b) and what reviewers expect. Explain the WAKE_UP gap before claiming the map model helps."
  }
 ],
